  "transcript": "# Maintenance and enforcement in public spaces\n\n## What do we mean by public space — and what's the committee's exam question?\n> Public space means where people live, move and meet; the committee should ask who is accountable and what would make maintenance reliable and visible.\n\nChair: Two framing questions: first, how do you define the public realm or public space? Second, what's the single 'exam question' this inquiry should answer?\nDavid Milner: We design streets, manuals and codes across the UK. 'Public realm' for me is the streets and spaces where people move and meet — pavements, shopfronts, trees, bins and signage.\nPatricia Brown: I see public realm as where we congregate and live our daily lives: roads, pavements, parks, play areas and the civic spaces that shape whether people choose to live and spend there.\nEd Lennox: Clean Up Britain focuses on litter and fly-tipping but that sits within the wider public realm: the maintenance, the visible care and the accountability for those places.\nChair: So the committee's exam question should focus on ownership and accountability for maintenance, and what practical steps create dependable, well-cared-for spaces?\n\n\n## How bad is it — perception, evidence and causes\n> People overwhelmingly feel public spaces are worse; causes are mixed design, weakened local leadership, complex responsibilities, underfunding and cultural change.\n\nChair: Are public spaces definitely worse, and how do we know?\nDavid Milner: I can't point to a single national deterioration dataset, but polling and repeated local feedback show pavement quality, bins and visual clutter are top concerns.\nEd Lennox: Surveys show public perception is strongly negative. We also see practical failures: bridges with tree roots, unrepaired roadworks and a huge number of fly-tips with few prosecutions.\nPatricia Brown: Many gains were made since the 1990s, but austerity, fragmented waste systems and the 'takeaway/leave-behind' culture eroded maintenance and public care.\nLord Cameron of Dillington: Does quality vary around the country and between rural and urban areas?\nEd Lennox: Yes — rural areas often have more fragmented ownership (parish, county, National Highways) so responsibility is disputed; it's simpler in urban areas but still inconsistent.\nPatricia Brown: There are great and poor examples everywhere; the difference is often accidental — the presence of strong local civic leadership or the reverse.\nDavid Milner: Regional differences also reflect funding and governance patterns: who holds the highways budget, whether there's devolved funding, and local entrepreneurial leaders.\n\n\n## Who is responsible — systems, culture and everyday competence\n> Responsibility is spread across too many bodies; people see visible failures as signs the state doesn't care. Rebuilding local agency and clearer duties matters.\n\nLord Bailey of Paddington: Do degraded spaces feed wider political narratives about neglect and care?\nPatricia Brown: Yes. Poorly maintained places tell residents they are not valued. That shapes political narratives and trust in local representatives.\nDavid Milner: First impressions matter — stations, streets and airports signal competence. Everyday 'street scars' — patched pavements, phone boxes, bins — show a lack of ownership.\nEd Lennox: The law makes dropping litter and fly-tipping criminal, but detection and penalties are minimal. People rarely get caught; fines are low and enforcement scarce.\nBaroness Miller of Chilthorne Domer: Dog fouling has improved. What lessons help change behaviour?\nEd Lennox: Behaviour change came from unceasing public messages and enforcement; we've lost consistent civic messaging. Education, starting in schools, matters hugely.\nDavid Milner: Design shapes culture: if streets are built for cars rather than walking, people won't take care. Amsterdam changed design first, then culture shifted.\nPatricia Brown: Agency matters: where people feel ownership they treat places better. Business improvement districts, heritage projects and local champions show how civic pride is revived.\nLord Porter of Spalding: Councils have tight budgets — 87% on social care etc. Is lack of money the main problem?\nEd Lennox: It's not just lack of money; it's poor spending and fragmented contracts. There's enough public money overall but not the local accountability and competence to spend it well.\n\n\n## Practical solutions: clearer accountability, local roles and quick wins\n> Practical steps include one clear local owner for public realm stretches, reviving local 'lengthspeople', simpler rules for community action, smarter enforcement and shining a light on good practice.\n\nBaroness Barran: If you could start a five-year plan, what are two or three catalytic actions?\nPatricia Brown: Create simplified local ownership — a single responsible body with budget and staff — and spotlight where it works so other places can copy it. Keep people 'on the streets' rather than outsourcing everything to cameras.\nEd Lennox: Three practical steps: 1) a ministerial champion for the public realm to coordinate nationally; 2) revive local lengthspeople who own stretches of place; 3) insist independent sign-off that roadworks and site clearances are properly completed before contractors are paid.\nDavid Milner: Lower regulatory barriers to local action — e.g., a 'right to plant' outside your home — and fix perverse incentives such as anachronistic low fines in the New Roads and Street Works Act 1991 that encourage slow, poor-quality reinstatement.\nLord Ravensdale: How do we move from perception to evidence so we can measure improvement?\nEd Lennox: There are national surveys (Keep Britain Tidy and others) and local reporting. We should collect consistent metrics on litter, fly-tips, roadwork reinstatement and maintenance backlogs and publish them.\nBaroness Griffin of Princethorpe: Any underexplored areas the committee should probe?\nPatricia Brown: Look at equity: map who lacks access to good public space and how management failures affect mobility-impaired or low-income communities; study long-term stewardship models, not one-off redesigns.\nEd Lennox: Ask the bigger exam question: how do we rebuild trust that public agencies will maintain the public realm? Simplify the number of agencies, and make them enablers for people who want to help.\nChair: We asked witnesses to send follow-up evidence. David had to leave for another committee but will return written notes; Patricia and Ed will provide more concrete case studies and costed ideas such as lengthspeople.\nPatricia Brown: One final practical note: small, well-maintained lighting and carefully designed spaces can change how people feel about a place — even one resident saying 'it makes me feel rich' shows the power of small investments in care.\nChair: Thank you. The inquiry will press on the central themes: clearer local accountability, simpler rules for civic action, consistent maintenance funding, and rebuilding civic pride through education and design.\n",
